-- | The precedence a declaration's literal spells, or `None` — plan 90.
-- |
-- | ```text
-- | "6"   -> Some 6
-- | "12"  -> None     -- outside the scale
-- | "6u8" -> None     -- a suffix, and `Int` carries it verbatim
-- | "1_0" -> None     -- a separator, likewise
-- | ```
-- |
-- | **A table of ten, not a parse.** `TokenKind::Int` carries its text verbatim
-- | *"including any `_` separators and type suffix"*, so anything that reads a
-- | number out of it has to decide what to do with those. A ten-arm table
-- | decides by not accepting them, which is the right answer here — a
-- | precedence is one digit on Haskell's scale — and it makes `RDX0124` one
-- | diagnostic for every way of writing something else.
-- |
-- | It also avoids two things the alternatives cost: `parse` wants a turbofish,
-- | which doc 03 §18 does not have, and `from_str_radix(…, 10)` is exactly what
-- | clippy's `from_str_radix_10` asks to be replaced *with* `parse`. The table
-- | is shorter than either argument.
export(crate) digit_precedence : &str -> Option<u8>
digit_precedence text = case text of
  "0" -> Some 0
  "1" -> Some 1
  "2" -> Some 2
  "3" -> Some 3
  "4" -> Some 4
  "5" -> Some 5
  "6" -> Some 6
  "7" -> Some 7
  "8" -> Some 8
  "9" -> Some 9
  _ -> None
